Description: 'Mplog-Parser: parses Microsoft Protection log files into CSV files'
Category: Antivirus
Author: Thomas DIOT (Qazeer)
Version: 1.1
Id: 6084c8ab-2059-41a4-89f4-dba2cfdb4bb4
BinaryUrl: https://github.com/Qazeer/mplog_parser-compiled/releases/download/v1.0/mplog_parser.exe
ExportFormat: csv
Processors:
    -
        Executable: vao_mplogparser.exe
        CommandLine: -d "%SourceDirectory%\C\ProgramData\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Support" -o "%destinationDirectory%"
        ExportFormat: csv

# LOCAL FIX 2026-08-09 (v1.0 -> v1.1): the upstream CommandLine was missing the drive-letter
# component. KAPE lays collected files out as <tdest>\C\ProgramData\... (verified against a real
# collection), so "%SourceDirectory%\ProgramData\..." pointed at a folder that never exists and
# the module produced NOTHING while exiting quietly. Measured: with the path as shipped ->
# exit 1, empty output; with \C\ added -> exit 0, 2 CSVs from a 17.6 MB MPLog in 1.2 s.
# BitsParser.mkape in this same folder already uses the \C\ form, which is what tipped me off.

# Documentation
# Mplog-Parser parses Microsoft Protection log files into a number of CSV files.
# mplog_parser source: https://github.com/Intrinsec/mplog_parser
# Compiled version: https://github.com/Qazeer/mplog_parser-compiled
# Information on Windows Defender MPLog:
# https://www.crowdstrike.com/blog/how-to-use-microsoft-protection-logging-for-forensic-investigations/
# https://www.intrinsec.com/hunt-mplogs/
# https://artefacts.help/windows_defender_support_logs.html
